 # Gradient Computation via Finite Differences
 
-Also called: Central Difference Gradient, Central Difference Gradient Operator, Central Difference Operator, Central Differences, Finite Difference Gradient, Volume Gradient Estimation (Central Differences)
+Also called: Central Difference Gradient, Central Difference Gradient Operator, Central Difference Operator, Finite Difference Gradient, Volume Gradient Estimation (Central Differences)
 
 ## Overview
-Gradient Computation via Finite Differences estimates the spatial derivative vector (surface normal) of a scalar volume field at discrete voxel positions to enable local illumination models such as Phong shading or directional lighting [S1, S3, unofficial][S4, book]. It differs from [Analytic Gradient Computation](2167-analytic-gradient-computation.md) by relying on discrete sampling rather than closed-form differentiation, and from [Gradient Estimation from Samples](2180-gradient- estimation-from-samples.md) by using a deterministic, grid-aligned stencil structure rather than stochastic or ray-based estimation [S1, S3, unofficial].
+Gradient Computation via Finite Differences estimates the spatial derivative vector (surface normal) of a scalar volume field at discrete voxel positions to enable local illumination models such as Phong shading or directional lighting [S1, unofficial][S3, unofficial][S4, book]. It differs from [Analytic Gradient Computation](2167-analytic-gradient-computation.md) by relying on discrete sampling rather than closed-form differentiation, and from [Gradient Estimation from Samples](2180-gradient-estimation-from-samples.md) by using a deterministic, grid-aligned stencil structure rather than stochastic or ray-based estimation [S1, unofficial][S3, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs:** A scalar volume $V$ of dimensions $W \times H \times D$ (width, height, depth), stored in row-major order (x fastest, y middle, z slowest) [S10, S12, unofficial]. Physical spacing $\Delta x, \Delta y, \Delta z$ between voxels [S29, book].
